  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 158 AM CDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\nHot and dry conditions will continue through tonight ahead of \nHurricane Isaias approach to the Gulf coast. Breezy conditions \ncombined with hot/dry conditions in place, there is a potential for \nfire weather concerns through tonight for the northern portions \nof the area. \n\nHurricane Isaias continues to move northeast in the central Gulf, \neventually turning northward towards the AL/FL coastline over the \nnext 24 to 36 hours. Isaias has strengthened to Cat. 2 at 104mph \nsustained winds with the satellite presentation improving and \npressure (975mb) continuing to deepen. GOES East infrared imagery \ncurrently indicate some increasing shear but a deep burst of \nconvection is  developing on the western side. Center of circulation \nwill remain mostly east of area of our forecast area, making \nlandfall in the FL Panhandle just east of the AL/FL state line. This \nlandfall location will keep greatest impacts further east. As the \ndecaying Tropical Storm Isaias tracks northeast through Alabama \nthrough Saturday morning and degrades into a tropical depression, a \nunique synoptic setup will present the potential for enhanced winds \nacross the northwestern counties and Delta region on the periphery \nof our CWA. The interaction between the decaying cyclone and the \nridge of high pressure, a tight pressure gradient will establish \nstrong northerly flow, sandwiching the wind field and creating a jet \nlike mechanism on the back side of the circulation. With a 40-50 kt \nlow-level jet like winds wrapping around the western flank, deep \nboundary mixing in the low-levels during the morning to early \nafternoon will allow for that to translate to the surface, \nmaximizing gust potential as well. A wind advisory will be likely \nfor portions of the area along the Mississippi River Valley with the \nnext forecast update. Tropical Storm Wind probs are in the low \ncategorical range (10 to 30 percent) but enough to support added \ntropical headlines. For this reason, a Tropical Storm Watch \ncontinues for Forrest, Jones, Jasper, Newton, Neshoba, Winston, \nNoxubee, Kemper, Lauderdale, Clarke, Oktibbeha, Clay and Lowndes \nCounties through Saturday evening. An Elevated was added earlier \nareawide to the HWO graphic for gusty winds, due to that better \nsupport in the CAMs and high- resolution ensemble wind probs for \ngusty winds. Wind gusts over 40 mph will be possible across the Pine \nBelt as early as 4-5 pm this evening.\n\n\nFlash flooding is also possible starting late tonight through \nSaturday night. Expected totals are 2-4 inches of rain in north \ncentral, northeast to east MS while less in the 1-3 inch range in \nnorthwest, central to southeast MS. This is also due to warm cloud \nprocesses/tropical rain. As Isaias progress closer, additional \nupdates will be provided so please remain up to date with the latest \ninformation.",
